 {
  "files": [
   "capstone.webp"
  ],
  "title": "Pyramidion of Iufaa",
  "date": "Late Period, Dynasty 26, 664-525 BCE",
  "place": "probably Abydos",
  "medium": "Limestone, paint",
  "dimensions": "H 35.5 x W 27.2 x D 26.4 cm",
  "source": "The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Rogers Fund, 1921 (21.2.66), Open Access",
  "url": "https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/552805",
  "license": "CC0 1.0 (public domain)",
  "images": [
   "https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/LC-21_2_66_EGDP027851.jpg",
   "https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/LC-21_2_66_EGDP027848.jpg",
   "https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/LC-21_2_66_EGDP027849.jpg",
   "https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/LC-21_2_66_EGDP027850.jpg"
  ],
  "note": "Four faces, each from the Met view that looks straight at it (the four colour views), matted with BiRefNet, then each mapped by an affine fit of its own measured base corners and apex onto the true face triangle (slant / base 1.416 from the catalogue dimensions; the photos measure 1.36-1.38, i.e. the camera about 14 degrees above the face normal). The four black-and-white corner views were used to check which faces adjoin. The museum's painted accession number on face A was inpainted out. Mid-tones opened slightly (gamma 0.8): the Met shot it on black. Tile order in the atlas: A scarab in the solar barque, C Iufaa kneeling under the jackal (three columns), B sun disc between adoring baboons, D Iufaa kneeling under the jackal (two columns)."
 },
